						    <h1 class="page-title ">
        Measure P    </h1>

							
							
<div class="entry-content">
	<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>MEASURE P</strong><br />
<strong>CITY OF HERCULES</strong></p>
<p>Shall the measure providing Hercules with locally controlled funding to maintain financial stability/general city services, such as fast 911 response; neighborhood police patrols; water quality standards; keeping public areas/parks safe/ clean; repairing deteriorating streets/potholes; and retaining/attracting local businesses, by establishing a one-cent (1%) sales tax generating approximately $4,000,000 annually until ended by voters; requiring audits, public disclosure, and funds only for Hercules, be adopted?</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>CITY ATTORNEY’S IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE P</strong></p>
<p>This measure (the “Hercules Public Safety/ Financial Stability Measure”) would add Chapter 11 to Title 8 to the Hercules Municipal Code and would impose a transactions and use tax, commonly known as a sales tax, of up to 1% in the City of Hercules.</p>
<p>The tax would be imposed on the sale or use of tangible personal property in the City. The tax would be administered by the California Department of Tax and Fee Administration, which administers all sales and use taxes and transactions and use taxes in California.</p>
<p>The tax would be collected in addition to the existing sales and use and transactions and use taxes already imposed by the State of California, Contra Costa County, and BART in the City of Hercules. It would also be collected in addition to the City’s existing transactions and use tax in Chapter 9 of Title 8 of the Hercules Municipal Code. The current combined rate for all sales and use and transactions and use taxes in Hercules is 9.25%.</p>
<p>Groceries and prescription medicines would be exempt from the tax.</p>
<p>The proposed tax is estimated to generate $4,000,000 per year, and the California Constitution prohibits the State from taking this revenue from the City.</p>
<p>The proposed tax is a general tax, which means the revenues could be used by the City for any lawful municipal purpose, such as 911 response, neighborhood police patrols, water quality standards, keeping public areas and parks safe and clean, repairing deteriorating streets and fixing potholes, and retaining and attracting local businesses.</p>
<p>If approved, the tax would begin to be collected on April 1, 2027 and would continue to be collected until repealed.</p>
<p>The tax would be imposed at a rate of 1%, unless state law limits the tax rate to a lower rate, in which case it would be imposed at the maximum rate allowed under the law, but no more than 1%</p>
<p>Revenues collected under this tax will be subject to an independent financial audit conducted annually by a certified public accountant, and the expenditure of the revenues will be stated in the annual budget. Both the annual audit and the budget will be presented and reviewed by the Hercules City Council in an open public meeting.</p>
<p>The measure was placed on the ballot by the Hercules City Council. The measure will take effect if a majority (50% plus 1) of voters voting on the measure vote “Yes.”</p>
<p>s/Christine Crowl, City Attorney, City of Hercules</p>
<p>The above statement is an impartial analysis of Measure P. If you desire a copy of the measure, please call the office of the Hercules City Clerk at (510) 799-8208 and a copy will be mailed at no cost to you.</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE P</strong></p>
<p><strong>If you care about Public Safety and Health in Hercules, vote Yes on P!</strong></p>
<p>Hercules is finally a place where people want to live and raise their families. Voting Yes on P maintains our community momentum and the essential City services that make Hercules a safe, well-maintained place to live during a time of economic and national uncertainty.</p>
<p>Yes on P maintains a healthy, safe and vibrant Hercules community.</p>
<p>Yes on P maintains fast 911 response and the police officers patrolling our neighborhoods.</p>
<p>Yes on P helps our City meet current water quality standards.</p>
<p>Yes on P keeps public areas and parks safe and clean.</p>
<p>Yes on P retains and attracts local businesses.</p>
<p>With the State facing its own major budget deficit and declining federal funding, Measure P is a critical strategy to continue to maintain Hercules’s long-term financial stability. Measure P is fiscally accountable with mandatory financial audits, yearly reports, and public disclosure to ensure its funds are spent on the community’s priorities, responsibly.</p>
<p>All Measure P funding must stay here in Hercules for our own local needs. Not a dime can be taken by Sacramento politicians, the County, or federal government.</p>
<p>Here’s what Yes on P Won’t do:</p>
<p>Measure P is Not a tax on your property or home.</p>
<p>Measure P is Not applied to food purchased as groceries or prescription medication.</p>
<p><strong>Join the majority of the Hercules City Council and community leaders: Vote Yes on P: Protect our Public Safety and our Safe, Healthy Hercules!</strong></p>
<p>To read the official Measure P documents, visit herculesca.gov</p>
<p>Hercules Police Officer’s Association, Chris Costa, President<br />
Darrel “Jay” Tucker, President, Hercules Historical Society, Former Hercules Mayor and Council Member<br />
Richard Mitchell, Former Hercules Planning Commissioner<br />
Jo Ellen Marcotte, Member, Hercules Library Foundation</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE P</strong></p>
<p>Public safety deserves reliable funding, but supporters use police and 911 response to sell another tax without explaining why existing revenues are not enough.</p>
<p>Hercules already has a half-cent Measure B sales tax that was extended indefinitely, plus an 8% utility users tax continued indefinitely by Measure N. Before asking families, seniors, and local businesses to pay more, City Hall should show what changed, what savings were pursued, and exactly how much additional police staffing Measure P will buy.</p>
<p>The State Controller reports that in 2025 Hercules had 34 Public Safety employees receiving $5,158,622 in wages plus $1,195,041 in benefits.</p>
<p>Some employees receive over $300,000 in total compensation, and retired police officers can get up to $6000 per year for health insurance on top of their pensions.</p>
<p>This is not an argument against police officers. It is a reason to demand a clear compensation, staffing, and long-term cost plan before approving another tax.</p>
<p>Supporters also point to audits and annual reports. Audits tell voters whether money was accounted for; they do not prove a tax is necessary, prevent future spending increases, or guarantee faster 911 response.</p>
<p>Exemptions for unprepared food and prescriptions do not make the tax painless. Working families still pay more for gas, clothes, school supplies, and new cars.</p>
<p>Remember, if both Measure P and the transit tax pass, we will be looking at a 10.75% total rate. How much is too much?</p>
<p>Protect public safety with responsible budgeting, not another tax increase.</p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure P.</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President<br />
Michael Hernandez, Hercules Resident<br />
Michael Letcher, Hercules Resident<br />
Kent Rummelhart, Hercules Resident</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE P</strong></p>
<p><strong>Vote No on the Measure P tax hike!</strong></p>
<p>Everything in and around Hercules costs more, and now the City Council wants to add another 1% to the price of gas, school supplies, clothes, and other essentials.</p>
<p><strong>A higher tax on nearly everything you buy:</strong></p>
<p>• Measure P raises Hercules’ sales tax from 9.25% to 10.25%. Your ballot also carries a separate 0.5% regional transit tax. If both pass, Hercules shoppers will pay 10.75%, tying El<br />
Cerrito and Pinole for the highest sales tax in Contra Costa County, and well above the national and California averages.</p>
<p>• It even raises the price of a new car. California charges vehicle tax at the buyer’s home address, so Hercules residents would pay 10.75% no matter where they buy, e.g., $5,375 on a<br />
$50,000 vehicle. You can’t escape it by shopping out of town.</p>
<p>• Sales taxes hit low- and middle-income families hardest. If Hercules truly values equity, why penalize those who can least afford it? If Measure P passes, shoppers can save 1.5%<br />
on necessities simply by buying in unincorporated Rodeo or El Sobrante.</p>
<p><strong>The real problem is spending, not revenue:</strong></p>
<p>• Hercules’ own audited statements show General Fund spending more than doubled in a decade, from $10.2 million in 2015 to $20.7 million in 2025. Where is the money going?</p>
<p>• Nine city employees each received more than $200,000 in wages in 2025 according to State Controller data.</p>
<p>• Staff admit the retail tax base is “not robust enough to fund anything more than basic service levels.” If spending can double while services stay “basic,” the problem isn’t too little<br />
tax money; it’s how the money is managed.</p>
<p><strong>Before charging residents the highest sales tax in the county, the Council should show real spending restraint.</strong></p>
<p><strong>The sales tax in Hercules is high enough already.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure P.</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President<br />
Michael Hernandez, Hercules Resident<br />
Michael Letcher, Hercules Resident</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE P</strong></p>
<p>What does an organization led by a Walnut Creek resident know about Hercules? Nothing.</p>
<p>What did the Contra Costa Taxpayers’ Association do over the past decade, to reform and stabilize Hercules? Nothing.</p>
<p>Don’t be fooled!</p>
<p>The truth is, Hercules is now a place where our residents want to live and raise families because over the last 15 years, the people of Hercules fought past corruption, demanded local control, established a budget watchdog committee to monitor city spending, and approved voter measures designed to protect and maintain neighborhood police patrols, 911 response, parks, youth and senior services.</p>
<p>Measure P was developed after nearly a year of community input from hundreds of Hercules residents.</p>
<p>We all know bigger cities like San José and San Francisco get all the funding!</p>
<p>With federal and state governments reducing the funding small cities like Hercules rely on, our ongoing fiscal stability is at risk again.</p>
<p>Yes on P keeps funds local to protect Hercules’s long-term fiscal sustainability, water quality, public safety, parks youth and senior services during a time of economic and national uncertainty.</p>
<p>Again:</p>
<p>Measure P isn’t a tax on your home or property</p>
<p>Measure P doesn’t apply to groceries or prescription medications</p>
<p>Please make an informed decision before you vote!</p>
<p>Join us &#8211;vote Yes on P for Hercules’s Public Safety, Parks and People.</p>
<p>Hercules Police Officer’s Association, Chris Costa, President<br />
Richard Mitchell, Former Hercules Planning Commissioner<br />
Jo Ellen Marcotte, Member, Hercules Library Foundation</p>
